meta {
  name: Rose
  type: collection
  version: 1
}

vars:pre-request {
  adminEmail: admin@aip.com
  adminPassword: admin
  locale: en
  legalPageKey: 1
}

docs {
# Rose API Collection

Use the `Local` environment for application requests. Its `baseUrl` already includes `/api`, so application URLs begin with `/app`.

## App request sequences

Customer registration is: **Register Request OTP** → **Register Verify OTP** → **Register Update Location**. Each response replaces `customerSessionToken`; the final step captures `customerAccessToken`. For an existing customer, use **Login Request OTP** → **Login Verify OTP** instead. **Resend OTP** is valid only while the current customer session represents the register or login OTP flow.

Provider registration is: **Get Categories** → **Register Request OTP** → **Register Verify OTP** → **Register Upload Documents**. Set `specialtyId` in the Local environment to an active category before registration; registration requires at least one unique, active category, a display image, National ID front and back images, and a selfie-verification image. The National ID request fields use `nationalId[]`, with front first and back second. Registration ends in pending status and does not issue a bearer token. For an active provider, use **Login Request OTP** → **Login Verify OTP**; the successful login captures `providerAccessToken`. **Resend OTP** is valid only while a provider register or login OTP session is active.

An active KYC-approved provider can add portfolio images one at a time using **Add Portfolio Photo**. The response captures the new `portfolioPhotoId`, which **Delete Portfolio Photo** uses to remove that image.

All OTP examples use `123456`, which the local API accepts for development. Request/response scripts keep customer and provider session, OTP, and bearer-token variables separate.

The Local environment uses currently seeded category, service, service-area, and replyable-review IDs. Replace an ID if your local database has been reseeded.

Customer review creation requires a bearer token and a completed booking owned by that customer which has not already been reviewed. Booking list and detail responses include `review.canCreate`; show the Rate & Review action only when it is `true`. A submitted review is returned in the same field. Provider replies require a bearer token for the provider who owns that review and can be submitted only once.

Customer booking creation is: **List Providers** → **Get Provider** → **Get Available Slots** → either **Create Booking - Saved Address** or **Create Booking - Map Pin** → **Get Booking Details**. Copy a marketplace-ready provider ID into the editable `providerId` Local-environment variable, then copy one of that provider's active service IDs into the editable `serviceId` variable. Run **Get Provider** to capture a matching same-category `secondServiceId` for that `serviceId`. The slots request captures the first two available timestamps for their combined duration. If `secondServiceId` remains empty, configure another active service in the first service's category before running the multi-service examples. For saved-address booking, run **List Addresses** or **Create Address** first so `addressId` is populated. Set `bookingDate` to a future enabled availability date before fetching slots. The two create requests are alternatives; when both are run, they use different captured slots.

**Get Provider** includes an active-review `reviews` section. It contains the reviewer's name and image, rating, review text, and creation date, with pagination metadata. Use optional `reviewsPage` and `reviewsPageSize` query parameters (defaults: `1` and `10`) to load further reviews.

New booking requests contain no payment data. They start with booking `status` and request `requestStatus` set to `pending`; the details endpoint includes `requestExpiresAt` and `remainingSeconds`. If the provider does not respond before the configured timeout, subsequent details responses show `status: cancelled` and `requestStatus: expired`.

Provider booking management is: **Get All Booking Requests** → **Get Booking Details** → **Update Booking Request Status** → **Get All Bookings**. The requests call captures the first pending `bookingId`. Set `providerBookingRequestStatus` to `accepted` or `rejected` before updating it. Accepted requests become `upcoming`; rejected requests become `cancelled`. The provider bookings list accepts `upcoming`, `in_progress`, or `completed` as `providerBookingListStatus`.

Providers can cancel only their own upcoming bookings through **Cancel Booking**. The request requires one of: `personal_emergency`, `health_issue`, `equipment_or_supply_issue`, `scheduling_conflict`, `unable_to_reach_location`, or `other`; `comments` are optional. Booking responses include `canCancel` for showing the cancellation action.

## Current app enums

| Field | Values |
| --- | --- |
| `locale` | `en`, `ar` |
| customer `gender` | `1` male, `2` female, `3` other |
| service `gender` | `0` both, `1` men, `2` women |
| legal `pageKey` (customer) | `1` terms, `2` privacy, `3` cookie |
| legal `pageKey` (provider) | customer values plus `4` provider agreement |
| provider explore `sortBy` | `1` topRated, `2` nearest, `3` lowestPrice, `4` mostReviewed |
| availability `bufferMinutes` | `15`, `30`, `45`, `60` |
| customer address `label` | `1` home, `2` work, `3` other |
| booking `status` | `pending`, `upcoming`, `in_progress`, `completed`, `cancelled` |
| booking `requestStatus` | `pending`, `accepted`, `rejected`, `expired` |

Availability updates must include each of `monday` through `sunday` exactly once. Times use 24-hour `HH:mm`; enabled days require a start and end time with the end later than the start, while disabled days may use `null` times.
}
